//? Title: Posterior Collapse: Measure Latent Use Before Claiming a Cure
//? Subtitle: Matched sentence-VAEs, explicit KL warmup and signed empirical-mixture diagnostics
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A recurrent decoder can predict from true previous tokens while using little information from
its global latent code. We compare a constant KL weight with an eighteen-step linear warmup
in the same compact sentence VAE. Both runs use all 432 local grammar sentences, identical
initial weights, batches and Gaussian banks, three epochs/27 explicit Adam updates each,
and the same four-draw unweighted validation criterion. Both select epoch 3. Test negative
ELBO is 18.680923 nats/sentence for constant weight and 18.687373 for warmup. Neither run has
an active mean coordinate at the prespecified variance threshold. Fixed-prefix code ablations,
signed finite-mixture density estimates and unfiltered free decoding expose what these
numbers do and do not establish. This single short teaching run supplies no evidence that
warmup fixes collapse or learns useful language semantics.
)
\toc

> Two paths into an autoregressive decoder

A global Gaussian code is useful only if the decoder uses its information. Under teacher
forcing, the decoder also sees the **true previous target tokens**. Those prefixes can support
prediction while the code remains weak. [Bowman et al. (2016)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.06349)
study sentence VAEs and KL cost annealing. Our fixed vocabulary 19/embedding 8/GRU16/code 4 model
is a small teaching adaptation, with separate encoder/decoder embeddings and no word dropout,
not their language-corpus training recipe.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/collapse_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram          d(44, 1500);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle st;
  st.width     = 4.5;
  st.height    = 2.5;
  st.font_size = 20;
  d.node("x", {0, 0}, "Target tokens x\nPAD masked", st)
      .node("enc", {6, 0}, "Encoder GRU16\nEmbedding8", st)
      .node("q", {12, 0}, "q(z|x)\nGaussian code4", st)
      .node("init", {18, 0}, "Sample z\ntanh(dense(z))", st)
      .node("dec", {24, 0}, "Decoder GRU16\nSeparate embedding8", st)
      .node("pred", {30, 0}, "Next-token logits\nMasked NLL", st)
      .node("prefix", {18, -5}, "BOS then previous\nTRUE target", st)
      .node("prior", {12, 5}, "Standard normal prior\nbeta times KL cost", st)
      .edge("x", "enc")
      .edge("enc", "q")
      .edge("q", "init")
      .edge("init", "dec")
      .edge("dec", "pred")
      .edge("prefix", "dec")
      .edge("q", "prior")
      .text(
          {15, -9},
          "At fixed z, future prefixes cannot affect earlier logits.\nFree generation feeds "
          "emitted tokens back instead of true prefixes.",
          21)
      .save_svg("build/collapse_method.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/collapse_method.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The decoder has a latent initialization and a separate previous-token path. A connected latent path does not establish useful information transfer.)
\alttext(Targets feed a masked GRU encoder and Gaussian code. A sampled code initializes the decoder, which independently receives BOS then previous true targets and predicts the next token.)

PAD 0 leaves the encoder state unchanged. EOS 2 is a normal nonzero encoder token; reconstruction
loss includes EOS and excludes PAD. Decoder inputs are BOS 1 followed by previous targets,
never the current target. The global code enters only the decoder's initial state. All 22
trainable leaves/3,243 scalars and all recurrent/noise gradients remain live.

For per-sentence masked categorical NLL N_b and diagonal-Gaussian KL K_b, training uses

$$
J_\beta=\frac 1B\sum_b(N_b+\beta K_b),\qquad
K_b=\frac 12\sum_j(\mu_{bj}^2+e^{l_{bj}}-1-l_{bj}).
$$
\alttext(Training averages sentence NLL plus beta times the coordinate-summed analytic Gaussian KL. It does not average NLL over time or compare different beta objectives as ELBOs.)

NLL sums non-PAD token losses before averaging sentences. Beta 0 removes the KL contribution,
while beta 1 gives the ordinary negative ELBO. Comparing training objectives with different
beta does not compare like-for-like evidence bounds.

> A hand-checkable density decomposition

The empirical posterior mixture over N cases is qbar(z)=N^−1 sum_i q_i(z). At an aligned
point z_i, the exact log-ratio identity is

$$
\log\frac{q_i(z_i)}{p(z_i)}=
\log\frac{q_i(z_i)}{\bar q(z_i)}+\log\frac{\bar q(z_i)}{p(z_i)}.
$$
\alttext(The conditional posterior-to-prior log ratio decomposes into a posterior-to-mixture term plus a mixture-to-prior term at the same point.)

[Hoffman and Johnson's ELBO surgery](https://approximateinference.org/archives/2016/accepted/HoffmanJohnson 2016.pdf)
provides the decomposition perspective. Here every evaluated point uses the **same complete
finite mixture**, including its own posterior. Self-inclusion and finite-sample bias matter:
the sampled information/aggregate terms are not population mutual information, a true
aggregate KL, or semantic scores. Signed estimates are retained; analytic KL generally differs
from the sampled conditional log ratio by Monte Carlo error.

For two one-dimensional posteriors N(−2,1),N(2,1), mean analytic KL is 2 and variance of the
posterior means is 4. Evaluate at the two means, explicitly choosing z_i=mu_i:
the conditional log ratio is 2, the information term is log 2−log(1+exp(−8)), and the aggregate
term is the difference. These are **fixed density evaluation points, not random posterior
draws**. The actual experiment below separately retains four Gaussian draws in its synthetic
controls and fitted diagnostics.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/collapse_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n   = dm::nn;
  namespace m   = n::models;
  const auto t  = n::DType::Float64;
  const auto mu = n::from_vector(std::vector<double>{-2, 2}, {2, 1}), zero = n::zeros({2, 1}, t);
  const auto c          = m::CollapseStatistics(zero, zero, {zero}),
             a          = m::CollapseStatistics(mu, zero, {mu});
  const double expected = std::log(2.) - std::log1p(std::exp(-8.));
  if (std::abs(a.analytic_kl - 2) > 1e-14 || std::abs(a.mixture_information - expected) > 1e-14 ||
      a.active_units != 1)
    return 1;
  std::print(
      "| fixed control | analytic KL | log q_i/p | log q_i/qbar | log qbar/p | active units "
      "|\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| collapsed | {:.9f} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} | {} "
      "|\n| separated | {:.9f} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} | {} |\n",
      c.analytic_kl,
      c.sampled_conditional_kl,
      c.mixture_information,
      c.aggregate_kl,
      c.active_units,
      a.analytic_kl,
      a.sampled_conditional_kl,
      a.mixture_information,
      a.aggregate_kl,
      a.active_units);
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.bars(
       {1, 2, 3, 4},
       {a.analytic_kl, a.sampled_conditional_kl, a.mixture_information, a.aggregate_kl},
       "separated means",
       dm::RGBA{37, 99, 235},
       .6)
      .points({1, 2, 3, 4}, {0, 0, 0, 0}, "collapsed reference", dm::RGBA{220, 38, 38}, 5)
      .x_ticks({1, 2, 3, 4}, {"analytic KL", "log q/p", "log q/qbar", "log qbar/p"})
      .y_limits(-.15, 2.2)
      .title("Fixed evaluation at posterior means / no random draws")
      .x_label("density quantity")
      .y_label("nats")
      .save_svg("build/collapse_controls.svg");
}
```
