\caption(The separated fixed-point control has analytic KL 2 and a nonzero information term. The collapsed standard-normal control has all ratios zero. They validate the calculation, not the fitted model.)
\alttext(Four bars show analytic KL 2, conditional log ratio 2, information about 0.693 and aggregate ratio about 1.307. Collapsed-control markers are at zero.)

> Match everything except the KL schedule

The constant schedule is beta=1. The warmup schedule is min(1,float(step)/float(17)) for
zero-based steps 0–26: steps 0–17 include both endpoints, then beta stays 1. Source Float32
rounding is intentional. Beta is calculated from the visible optimizer step; no counter is
hidden in the model. Each run has 27 updates, so both spend their final ten updates at beta 1.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/collapse_schedule.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::vector<double> step, constant, warm;
  for (int k = 0; k < 27; ++k) {
    step.push_back(k);
    constant.push_back(dm::nn::models::CollapseBeta(false, k));
    warm.push_back(dm::nn::models::CollapseBeta(true, k));
  }
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.lines(step, constant, "constant", dm::RGBA{37, 99, 235}, 2)
      .lines(step, warm, "warmup", dm::RGBA{220, 38, 38}, 2)
      .points({0, 17, 26}, {0, 1, 1}, "warmup endpoints", dm::RGBA{220, 38, 38}, 4)
      .title("Explicit KL weights / 27 optimizer steps")
      .x_label("zero-based optimizer step")
      .y_label("beta")
      .y_limits(-.05, 1.3)
      .save_svg("build/collapse_schedule.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/collapse_schedule.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Warmup reaches one at step 17 and stays there. Different early training objectives require a common validation criterion.)
\alttext(A horizontal constant-weight line at one and a linear warmup from zero at step 0 to one at step 17, remaining at one through step 26.)

> Native grammar data and the complete editable fit

The six factors have cardinalities 2×3×3×2×4×3=432. Article, colour, shape, verb and direction
are mandatory; the adverb factor is absent/quickly/slowly. Sentences have six or seven targets,
including EOS. Every split is balanced in each factor; residue
2*((colour+shape+adverb)%3)+(article+verb+direction)%2 assigns test 0, validation 1, fit 2–5,
producing 288/72/72 disjoint combinations. Native preparation orders source IDs increasingly;
the loader preserves any valid supplied ordering and regenerates every sentence, prefix,
factor and identity. This is exhaustive procedural teaching data, not a language benchmark.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto root  = dm::datasets::prepare_example_data("sequence-vae-assets");
  const auto pools = dm::nn::models::LoadSequenceVaeData(root + "/sequence_vae");
  std::print("| pool | rows | source ID | first sentence |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n");
  for (int k = 0; k < 3; ++k) {
    const auto& p = pools[k];
    std::print(
        "| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",
        k == 0   ? "fit"
        : k == 1 ? "validation"
                 : "test",
        p.tokens.size(0),
        p.source.to_vector<int>()[0],
        dm::nn::models::SequenceText(p.tokens, 0));
  }
  std::print(
      "\nAll432 sentences, fixed19-word vocabulary, EOS/PAD and previous-target prefixes verified. "
      "Factors/IDs are provenance, not network inputs.\n");
}
```
