\caption(The zoomed axis makes the small NLL changes visible. Code removal/shuffling changes probabilities, establishing structural dependence rather than useful semantics.)
\alttext(Two closely spaced lines show posterior, zero, shuffled and prior-code teacher NLL. Posterior codes have the lowest values, with small absolute differences.)

The constant run's posterior/zero/shuffled/prior teacher NLLs are 18.686699/18.732299/18.723430/18.753247;
warmup gives 18.695210/18.739297/18.732103/18.763855. Zero/shuffle maximum probability effects are
about 0.076/0.093 for constant and 0.076/0.091 for warmup. Such nonzero effects do not establish
semantic representations. The reported teacher token perplexities 16.404448/16.418986 condition
on observed prefixes and posterior codes; they are not unconditional language-model perplexity.

Free decoding supplies BOS, then the model's **own emitted tokens**. It stops emitted text at
EOS, pads the remaining IDs, and still retains all seven raw decoder score steps. Mean-code
greedy decoding is a plug-in reconstruction, not a posterior predictive mean. The following
are all ten prespecified held-out rows floor(k*71/9), for both schedules, without filtering
failed grammar, repetition or missing EOS.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  for (const std::string name : {"constant", "warmup"}) {
    std::ifstream f("build/posterior_collapse/" + name + "/examples.tsv");
    std::string   line;
    std::getline(f, line);
    std::print(
        "\n{} / all ten fixed held-out cases\n\n| case | source ID | original | free greedy(mu) | "
        "free posterior greedy |\n|---:|---:|---|---|---|\n",
        name);
    int rows = 0;
    while (std::getline(f, line)) {
      for (char& c : line)
        if (c == '\t')
          c = '|';
      std::print("|{}|\n", line);
      ++rows;
    }
    if (rows != 10)
      return 1;
  }
}
```
